  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: ClinPhys 2
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "During a Phase I clinical trial, you notice that a patient\u2019s white
    blood cell count has dropped a little below the normal range. The study guidelines
    say it does not require immediate treatment for this small change.\n\n<b>Physicians
    can make treatment changes at any time during the clinical trial based on their
    clinical judgement, even if it does not follow the study guidelines.</b>\n\nIs
    this <b>true</b> or <b>false</b>?"
  explanation: "While physicians are responsible for protecting participants\u2019
    safety throughout a clinical trial, they must also closely follow the study protocol.
    Unless a serious, immediate risk to a participant\u2019s health arises, physicians
    should not make changes on their own. Altering treatment without consulting the
    research team could affect the accuracy of the data, make the results harder
    to interpret, and even violate important regulatory standards that keep clinical
    research safe and ethical."
  correctAnswer: False
  incorrectAnswer1: True
  incorrectAnswer2: 
  incorrectAnswer3: 
  questionRole: Physician
  questionType: TrueFalse
  questionStage: Clinical
